WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.088
NULL values can change the population that
contributes to an aggregate so

00:00:04.112 --> 00:00:08.462
we need to think carefully about them In this
example we calculate

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:13.375
average completed length of stay An ongoing
admission has no discharge time

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.800
yet Because the discharge time is missing there is
no completed stay

00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:22.250
duration to calculate for that row The WHERE clause
removes rows where

00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:28.550
discharge_time is NULL For the remaining rows
PostgreSQL subtracts admit_time

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:33.350
from discharge_time converts the interval into
seconds changes that to days and

00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:37.825
then AVG calculates the mean stay length The result
is rounded to

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:44.762
two decimal places The query also returns COUNT
discharge_time as completed_admissions

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.788
This is useful because an average is much easier to
interpret when

00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:52.925
we know how many observations contributed to it A
key point is

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:57.162
that AVG ignores NULL expressions but relying on
that behavior without thinking

00:00:57.188 --> 00:01:01.662
can hide data-quality or population issues It is
often better to make

00:01:01.675 --> 00:01:05.450
the intended population explicit with WHERE and to
report the sample size

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:10.550
beside the average Whenever you calculate an average
ask two questions which

00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:14.650
rows are included and how many observations actually
contributed to the mean
